freestanding: no panic handler is defined
    A freestanding build has no operating system to report a crash to, so the
    program itself must say what happens when it panics (print to a serial port
    or screen, then halt).
    A kernel has no operating system to fall back on when it panics, so
    Plutonium makes the program choose what happens at compile time, and checks
    the choice: the runtime calls the handler directly, with nothing to
    register, so it works from the first instruction.
    Mark one function with //pu:panic:
        //pu:panic
        func onPanic(p *rt.Panic) { ... }
    (see "Panics in freestanding programs" in the Plutonium spec, section 17.4)
    (run "pluto explain E2580" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:1:1: this program starts tasks, uses channels or waits in pluto/sync, so it needs a scheduler, and none is bound: mark a package-level variable whose type implements rt.Scheduler with //pu:scheduler (language spec section 19.5; hosted builds bind one by default)
    This program starts tasks, uses channels or waits in pluto/sync, so it needs
    a scheduler, and none is bound. Hosted builds bind one by default; a
    freestanding program, such as a kernel, provides its own: a package-level
    variable marked //pu:scheduler whose type implements rt.Scheduler.
    Plutonium carries a scheduler only in programs that ask for one, and a
    kernel supplies its own, so a freestanding program must say which.
    Bind the kernel's scheduler:
        //pu:scheduler
        var scheduler kernelScheduler
    (see "The scheduler contract" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.5)
    (run "pluto explain E2593" for a longer explanation)
